      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{62df7cd83e007e467295c4e1|full_name} Very Poor on quest is already very optimized. Unlike Very Poor for PC, quest has strict uploading limits in place that prevent unoptimized avatars from being uploaded. The hard limit on 10mb compressed and 40mb uncompressed size ensures that there is a fine balance between poly count, texture memory, skinned mesh renders, material slots, and more. Unlike having a limit for each of these stats by themselves, the size limit ensures there is a balance between these stats. If the model is higher in one stat, it will be lower in another. \n\nThe current limits for Poor on quest are not realistic at all. Many avatar creators would opt to skip a quest version altogether rather than attempt to reach Poor on quest. To compare, an Excellent rated avatar on PC with 32,000 polys, 1 skinned mesh render, 4 material slots, and only 75 bones total is Very Poor on quest. \n\nAs a quest user, we are not enabling 80 avatars in an instance, regardless of their performance rank. With the current performance gating, PC users would be better off using a PC only Medium avatar than a Medium avatar with a quest version, because the quest version is going to be Very Poor, and thus not allowed in the instance. Quest users do not suffer from Very Poor avatars like PC users do. The upper bounds a Very Poor on PC can reach is ridiculous, whereas the bounds of a Very Poor Quest avatar are controlled and restricted. This is why many mnay many clubs and groups set their rules to allow Very Poor quest avatars while having a restriction of Poor or Medium on PC. Many clubs that use these common guidelines cannot use the performance gating feature.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
